## Solution

I’m not an expert on Gemini, so there might be some misunderstanding, but unlike Anthropic/OpenAI providers, the Google provider doesn’t have a function-calling stop reason. Instead, the SDK simulates it by processing chunks appropriately. However, because we check for a tool call via the `hasToolCall` variable, if a single chunk contains both a `finishReason` and a function call, the code that determines `finishReason` runs first, inadvertently setting it to `stop`.

By reversing the order and handling function calls first, I resolve the issue.
The fix was to ensure that function-calling logic runs before setting the final `finishReason`.

This PR adds the ability to configure [dynamic retrieval](https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/grounding?lang=python#dynamic-retrieval) when using Google's search grounding.

Here's an example on how to use it:

```ts
import { google } from '@ai-sdk/google';
